  "concerns": [
    {
      "id": "def-lattice-path-and-step-set",
      "concern": "The Remark says S^n is finite exactly when S is finite. At n=0, S^0 is the singleton empty-word set even when S is infinite, so the claim is false as written.",
      "severity": "would-be-fatal"
    },
    {
      "id": "lem-sign-reversing-involution-on-intersecting-path-systems",
      "concern": "The claimed canonical tail swap is not generally an involution under its least-i, least-j rule. For example take P1:(0,0)→(3,0) along EEE, P2:(0,1)→(3,1) along EEE, and P3:(1,−1)→(2,1) along NNE. Initially the selected pair is P1,P3 at (1,0); after swapping, the new P1 follows P3's tail and meets P2 at (1,1), so the next selected pair is P1,P2 rather than the original pair.",
      "severity": "would-be-fatal"
    },
    {
      "id": "thm-lindstrom-gessel-viennot",
      "concern": "Its cancellation step depends entirely on the preceding sign-reversing involution, whose selection rule fails on a concrete three-path system. The determinant identity therefore lacks a valid local cancellation proof until that construction is replaced.",
      "severity": "would-be-fatal"
    },
    {
      "id": "lem-a-triangulation-has-a-unique-triangle-on-the-closing-side",
      "concern": "Step 3.1 omits the case of a diagonal {p,m} with p<k. The written second case instead repeats p=1<k<q<m and incorrectly says q contradicts the minimality defining k. Replacing it by q=m and p<k closes the argument immediately.",
      "severity": "gap-a-reader-closes"
    },
    {
      "id": "def-shattering-and-vc-dimension",
      "concern": "The well-definedness paragraph says every trace on the empty set is empty. For a nonempty family the trace is {∅}, which equals P(∅); the missing outer braces make the stated equality false.",
      "severity": "would-be-fatal"
    },
    {
      "id": "lem-the-down-shift-creates-no-new-shattered-set",
      "concern": "The j∈T case merely asserts that considering traces A and A∪{j} shows the original family realises both. The interaction between an actually shifted preimage and an unshifted preimage is not established; the standard compression argument needs that case split.",
      "severity": "gap-a-reader-closes"
    },
    {
      "id": "cor-sauer-shelah-polynomial-bound",
      "concern": "Step 2 applies binom(n,i)≤n^i only in its stated range i≤n, while the sum runs through i=d without assuming d≤n. The i>n terms are zero and a short range split repairs the proof.",
      "severity": "gap-a-reader-closes"
    },
    {
      "id": "lem-grid-reduction-of-a-multivariate-polynomial",
      "concern": "The Statement permits an empty S_i. Then g_i=1 and reduction gives the zero polynomial, but the page's degree definition leaves every degree of the zero polynomial undefined, so the required inequality deg_{x_i}(tilde f)<|S_i|=0 is not a well-formed conclusion.",
      "severity": "would-be-fatal"
    },
    {
      "id": "def-multilinear-polynomial-and-multilinear-reduction",
      "concern": "The definition calls f multilinear when all variable degrees are at most 1, but the cited degree convention leaves degrees of the zero polynomial undefined. Its asserted equivalent monomial-span definition includes zero, so the two clauses disagree on the zero polynomial.",
      "severity": "would-be-fatal"
    },
    {
      "id": "lem-an-unshattered-set-reduces-a-monomial-on-a-family",
      "concern": "The Statement promises an F-linear combination but quantifies no field F; the symbol F is simultaneously used for members of the set family. The claim is not typed as a standalone proposition.",
      "severity": "would-be-fatal"
    },
    {
      "id": "thm-sauer-shelah-by-multilinear-polynomials",
      "concern": "Fact [L1] says every function on the cube agrees with a unique multilinear polynomial, but the cited item only says every polynomial has a unique multilinear representative with the same cube values. Existence for an arbitrary function is a stronger interpolation statement not supplied by the citation.",
      "severity": "would-be-fatal"
    },
    {
      "id": "thm-frankl-wilson-nonuniform-bound",
      "concern": "Step 3 applies a bilinear-form criterion to polynomials paired with evaluation points without defining a common vector space or bilinear form. The cited triangular criterion concerns vectors v_i,w_j in one bilinear space, not a family of functions and evaluation functionals.",
      "severity": "would-be-fatal"
    },
    {
      "id": "ex-the-nonuniform-intersection-bound-on-the-pairs-in-four-points",
      "concern": "The stated polynomial for {0,1} is wrong. With L={0,1} and |F_i|=2, the theorem constructs ⟨x,v⟩(⟨x,v⟩−1), not 1−⟨x,v⟩; the displayed polynomial also does not vanish on disjoint later pairs.",
      "severity": "would-be-fatal"
    },
    {
      "id": "fs-fisher-inequality-holds-over-the-two-element-field",
      "concern": "The proposed eight-set Eventown witness contains ∅, while the false claim being refuted requires all sets to be nonempty. Removing ∅ leaves seven sets on six points and repairs the witness, but the written refutation does not satisfy its own hypothesis.",
      "severity": "would-be-fatal"
    },
    {
      "id": "ex-the-four-vertex-path-is-the-only-prime-graph-on-four-vertices",
      "concern": "Fact [L3] claims an exhaustive classification of connected four-vertex graphs but cites only the definition of standard complete, bipartite, path and cycle graphs. That dependency contains no classification theorem, so the proof's exhaustive step is unsupported.",
      "severity": "would-be-fatal"
    },
    {
      "id": "ex-substitution-into-an-edge-and-into-a-nonedge",
      "concern": "Substituting G2 for one vertex of K2 leaves the other vertex as a single vertex, not as an arbitrary copy of G1. Producing G1∨G2 or G1⊔G2 requires replacing both template vertices or starting from a host already containing G1; the construction stated is ill-defined.",
      "severity": "would-be-fatal"
    },
    {
      "id": "ex-the-modular-decomposition-of-a-blown-up-five-cycle",
      "concern": "The proof invokes the connected-anticonnected branch of Gallai without proving the blow-up has either property, and its claim that any larger module projects to a quotient module requires showing it is a union of whole blown-up parts. Neither move is supplied.",
      "severity": "would-be-fatal"
    },
    {
      "id": "ex-counting-induced-three-vertex-paths-by-extensions",
      "concern": "The extension accounting is false: after deleting an endpoint of P3, both orientations of the middle edge 12 extend, while only one orientation of each end edge 01 and 23 extends. The listed 1+1+0+0+1+1 reaches 4 for the wrong fibres.",
      "severity": "would-be-fatal"
    },
    {
      "id": "rem-conventions-for-prime-graphs",
      "concern": "The Remark says all listed conventions agree from two vertices onward, but it has just included conventions that reserve 'prime' for graphs on at least four vertices. Those conventions disagree at orders two and three.",
      "severity": "would-be-fatal"
    },
    {
      "id": "def-directional-and-weak-sparsity-between-vertex-sets",
      "concern": "The definition assumes X and Y are disjoint nonempty sets, then says 'when X=Y' weak sparsity becomes self-density. That case is excluded by its own domain.",
      "severity": "would-be-fatal"
    },
    {
      "id": "lem-a-self-regular-set-of-intermediate-density-carries-many-induced-copies",
      "concern": "The cited induced counting lemma requires every W_i to have size at least N(H,η). The local Statement omits |W|≥N(H,η), and [L1] restates the cited theorem without this load-bearing hypothesis.",
